Officials’ Handbook - 23.11.2025 

6. Elections 
Volt coordinates election campaigns. During these campaigns, candidates and Elected 
Officials carry specific responsibilities.  

6.1. Up for (Re-)Election 
Candidates are encouraged to work on the following tasks and responsibilities leading up to 
the elections together with the responsible Campaign Team: 

●​ Setting up a personal campaign team for textual, online, and practical support;  
●​ Drafting a personal strategy with campaign objectives; 
●​ Attending campaign calls; 
●​ Being proactive and transparent about campaign activities; 
●​ Arranging transportation to and from activities; 
●​ Increasing visibility and influence; 
●​ Building (thematic) networks; 
●​ Organising and/or attending fundraising events; 
●​ Mapping and managing (possible) donors. 

6.2. Final Mandate 
If an Elected Official has decided not to run for re-election, they should support their possible 
successor(s) in their election. This includes: 

●​ Starring in campaign events or videos; 
●​ Endorsing and being visible with possible successor(s); 
●​ Sharing best practice on the life and tasks of an Elected Official. 

6.3 Coalitions 
In certain electoral systems, forming or joining broader Coalitions (§2.5 Officials’ Code of 
Conduct of the Integrity Syllabus) that align with Volt’s values may be politically necessary or 
strategically beneficial to advance shared objectives. However, participation in such Coalitions 
must always safeguard Volt’s core principles, and independence. Before joining any Coalition, 
the following steps must be taken: 

●​ Engage in a conversation with the leading candidate or an official representative of 
the potential alliance; 
●​ Evaluate both the content of that discussion and the alliance’s official manifesto or 
programme; 
●​ Conduct a joint assessment with the EOO regarding Volt’s potential support or 
participation. 

If at any point the alliance, or its representatives, act contrary to Volt’s values or 
commitments, Volt should promptly reconsider, or withdraw, from the cooperation. 
